# AmorphDB PWA — Reference Signup Watcher
#
# Reference implementation shipped with the AmorphDB PWA boilerplate.
# `amorphctl init-pwa` copies this file into new projects (Phase C4 of
# docs/pwa_auth_development_plan.md) and substitutes the placeholder app
# name `myapp` with the project's chosen app name.
#
# Behavior:
#   - Observes the pre-auth devices subtree.
#   - When a browser writes device.signup.submitted = true, looks up the
#     credentials at world.apps.myapp.auth.credentials.<username>.
#   - If the username is already taken: writes
#     { status: "error", message: "User already exists" } to
#     device.signup.result and stops.
#   - Otherwise: hashes the password with Argon2id, stores the hash and
#     salt under auth.credentials.<username>, marks the user's data
#     subtree as shown, calls the app-provided setup_user_watchers
#     procedure to register per-user watchers, generates an auth token,
#     installs it under tokens.<token>, and writes
#     { status: "ok", token: token } to device.signup.result. This is
#     the auto-login step — the browser stores the returned token and
#     proceeds to the authenticated app on the next request.
#
# The Go bridge detects device.signup.result and forwards the parsed JSON
# back to the browser (Step A4 of docs/pwa_auth_development_plan.md).
# The browser stores the token in localStorage and presents it on
# subsequent MCP requests via the X-AmorphDB-Token header (Phase B2).
#
# Apps that need OAuth, SAML, SSO, invite-only signup, or email
# verification replace this watcher entirely. The hand-off shape (writing
# to tokens.<token> with .identity and .device, and to
# users.<username>._shown = true) and the platform's write boundary are
# unchanged — only the credential creation logic differs. See
# docs/pwa_identity_auth_draft.md for OAuth and SSO examples.
#
# Configuration read at runtime:
#   world.apps.myapp.app._token_ttl  — token lifetime in seconds, or 0
#                                      to never expire. Set by the
#                                      boilerplate from the token_ttl
#                                      attribute on the root <section>
#                                      in app.html.
#
# Application contract:
#   my.app.setup_user_watchers(username) — procedure provided by the
#       application (typically in setup.mbl). Called once per new user
#       to register the per-user watchers that handle that user's
#       intents. The reference watcher invokes it after credentials are
#       written and before the auto-login token is issued, so the new
#       user's watchers are live by the time their first authenticated
#       request arrives.

watch my.app.auth.signup_handler(world.apps.myapp.devices):
    for device in world.apps.myapp.devices:
        if device.signup.submitted = true:
            username = device.signup.username
            password = device.signup.password

            existing = world.apps.myapp.auth.credentials[username]
            if not is_unknown(existing):
                device.signup.result = { status: "error",
                                         message: "User already exists" }
            else:
                hashed = my.computer.crypto.hash_password(password)
                world.apps.myapp.auth.credentials[username].password_hash = hashed.hash
                world.apps.myapp.auth.credentials[username].salt = hashed.salt

                world.apps.myapp.users[username]._shown = true

                my.app.setup_user_watchers(username)

                token = my.computer.crypto.generate_token()
                ttl = world.apps.myapp.app._token_ttl

                world.apps.myapp.tokens[token].identity = username
                world.apps.myapp.tokens[token].device = device
                if ttl > 0:
                    world.apps.myapp.tokens[token].@ttl = ttl

                device.signup.result = { status: "ok", token: token }

            device.signup.submitted = (quietly) false
